{{!-- One move row body (shared between the major-arcana card and the rows a choice grants).
      Context is a MoveSnapshot; params: categoryKey, alwaysShowResource, `disclosure` and
      `startOpen`. The caller supplies the <li>/<ol> wrapper. No acquisition box: the rails' and the
      tabs' moves are drawn by move-list-row.hbs, which has the one a Moves panel chooses with.

      `startOpen` renders the disclosure already open — for a surface whose whole point is the move's
      text, like the season the turn is about to roll. The reader can still shut it, and
      OpenDisclosures remembers that they did: what it stores is what the reader CHANGED, so the
      template is still what decides where a region starts.

      `disclosure` collapses the row: the name becomes a button that opens the move's text in flow,
      and while it is shut the row says what the move is FOR — its own trigger, as a gloss. Six moves
      printed in full is 1400px of a column; six rows is a list you can read.

      The row keeps the SAME controls either way — the die and the chat bubble, in the same places,
      doing the same things. A disclosure changes what the row SAYS, not what it can do, so it is not
      a second vocabulary to learn. Opening the move's own item sheet is dropped from a disclosure
      row on purpose: the body already IS the move's text, which is what that button was for.

      Activation, not proximity. A hover card would be governed by SC 1.4.13 (dismissible, hoverable,
      persistent) and would need a second path for touch and a third for the keyboard; a disclosure
      announces itself through `aria-expanded` and behaves identically for all three. Rows shifting
      below the one you open was an objection to HOVER — once the trigger is a click, the shift is
      what the reader just asked for.

      They are independent, not an accordion: opening one never closes another. --}}
<div class="stonetop-item-header">
	{{!-- The name opens the move's own item sheet, the way the Items sidebar does. A <button>, not a
	      <strong>: core binds click only for [data-action], so anything else is keyboard-dead.
	      data-view-state keeps it live on a locked sheet — reading a move mutates nothing.

	      As a disclosure it opens the text in place instead. The button is the NAME alone: the gloss
	      is the row's second line and ends the header (see below), so it runs the row's full width
	      rather than stopping where the controls begin — and the button's accessible name stays
	      "Bolster" instead of the move's whole trigger read aloud after it. Opening the move's own
	      sheet is then a button in the body. --}}
	{{!-- A move the book prints with no name draws none: the row is the move's own words. It still HAS
	      a name — the roll button and the chat card below say it — so nothing here goes unannounced. A
	      disclosure row is the exception: there the name IS the control that opens the text, so a
	      nameless move keeps it rather than collapsing behind nothing.

	      Dropping the name is not enough on its own. The header is a LABEL line, and emptied of its
	      label it still took a line — so the die and the chat bubble stood on a blank strip above the
	      paragraph they act on, reading as decoration belonging to nothing. The row says so with a
	      class (move-row.hbs) and the CSS puts them on the move's own first line instead. --}}
	{{#if (and nameless (not disclosure))}}
	{{else if disclosure}}
	<button type="button" class="stonetop-item-name stonetop-move-disclosure" data-action="toggleMoveBody"
	        data-disclosure data-view-state data-move-slug="{{slug}}"
	        aria-expanded="{{#if startOpen}}true{{else}}false{{/if}}"
	        aria-controls="{{moveBodyId @root.sheetIdPrefix categoryKey slug}}">
		<i class="fas fa-caret-right stonetop-move-caret" aria-hidden="true"></i>
		<span class="stonetop-move-disclosure-name">{{name}}</span>
	</button>
	{{else}}
	<button type="button" class="stonetop-item-name stonetop-item-name--open" data-action="openMove"
	        data-view-state data-move-slug="{{slug}}"
	        title="{{localize 'stonetop.character.moves.openSource'}}">{{name}}</button>
	{{/if}}
	{{!-- The caption beside the name — an arcanum move's "Requires: Battery". Never on a disclosure
	      row: line two there is the gloss, and a caption ahead of it stands between the move's name
	      and the move's own words. --}}
	{{#if (and sourceLabel (not disclosure))}}<span class="stonetop-item-source">{{sourceLabel}}</span>{{/if}}
	{{!-- On a disclosure row the controls are one group, so they can hold the first line together
	      while the gloss runs the full width underneath them. Opened and closed around the same
	      controls both variants render, so there is still one set of them.

	      A nameless row groups them for the same reason and a different layout: with no name there is
	      no label line, so the group is floated onto the move's OWN first line rather than left
	      standing on an empty one above it. Either way it is one group, so it can be placed as one
	      thing — see .stonetop-item--nameless in the CSS. --}}
	{{#if (or disclosure nameless)}}<span class="stonetop-item-controls">{{/if}}
	{{!-- Any move that rolls shows its die, ticked or not: the check marks what the character has
	      taken, not what the table is allowed to roll. --}}
	{{!-- A <button>, not a <span>: the die is a control, so it has to be reachable by the keyboard and
	      look like one. It is styled exactly as the steading's rating die (`.steading-roll-die`) —
	      faint at rest, the accent under a pointer — so the two dice on one sheet mean one thing. --}}
	{{!-- The slug rides along so a move the ACTOR DOES NOT OWN still rolls as itself: the row's
	      `.item[data-item-id]` is how an owned move is found, and a granted move has no such id. --}}
	{{#if rollStat}}
	<button type="button" class="rollable move-rollable" data-roll="{{rollStat}}" data-mod=""
	        data-move-slug="{{slug}}"
	        title="{{localize 'stonetop.a11y.rollMove' name=name}}"
	        aria-label="{{localize 'stonetop.a11y.rollMove' name=name}}">
		<i class="fas fa-dice-d6" aria-hidden="true"></i>
	</button>
	{{/if}}
	<button type="button" class="stonetop-move-chat" data-action="moveToChat" data-move-slug="{{slug}}"
	   title="{{localize 'stonetop.character.moves.sendToChat'}}" aria-label="{{localize 'stonetop.character.moves.sendToChat'}}">
		<i class="fas fa-comment"></i>
	</button>
	{{#if (and (or alwaysShowResource (gt selection.value 0)) resource)}}
	<span class="stonetop-item-resources">
		{{!-- moveSlug is what the pip handler reads; slug is passed explicitly because a partial
		      inherits the caller's context, and an attribute nobody asked for is worse than one
		      that is spelled out. --}}
		{{> "stonetop.resource-track" resource=resource btnClass="stonetop-item-resource-check"
		    action="moveResourcePip" moveSlug=slug slug=slug}}
		{{> "stonetop.resource-input" input=resource.input moveSlug=slug}}
	</span>
	{{/if}}
	{{#if (or disclosure nameless)}}</span>{{/if}}
	{{!-- The row's second line, and the last thing in the header so it wraps under everything above
	      it rather than under the name alone. No dash in front of it: it is its own line, not a
	      continuation of the name. --}}
	{{#if disclosure}}{{#if (hasText gloss)}}<span class="stonetop-move-gloss">{{gloss}}</span>{{/if}}{{/if}}
</div>
{{#if requirement}}<div class="stonetop-item-requirement{{#unless requirement.met}} stonetop-item-requirement--unmet{{/unless}}">{{localize "stonetop.character.moves.requires"}}: {{requiresLabel}}{{#unless requirement.met}} ({{localize "stonetop.character.moves.notMet"}}){{/unless}}</div>{{/if}}

{{!-- Everything below is the same content in both variants; a disclosure just wraps it in the region
      its button controls. `hidden` rather than a class, because a hidden region leaves the tab order
      and the accessibility tree for free — which is exactly what `aria-expanded="false"` promises. --}}
{{#if disclosure}}<div class="stonetop-move-body" id="{{moveBodyId @root.sheetIdPrefix categoryKey slug}}" {{#unless startOpen}}hidden{{/unless}}>{{/if}}

<div class="stonetop-item-description">{{rich description}}</div>

{{#if choices}}
<div class="stonetop-move-choices" data-move-slug="{{slug}}">
	{{#each choices.list}}
	{{> "stonetop.choice-row" cgContext="move" cgGroup=../choices.slug namePrefix=../slug}}
	{{/each}}
</div>
{{/if}}

{{#if disclosure}}</div>{{/if}}
